/**
 * CLOUDFLARE WORKERS DEPLOYMENT.
 *
 * This site is NOT static and cannot be deployed as one. It has API
 * routes, server components, server-side pricing calculation that must
 * never reach the browser, admin authentication, and a service-role
 * Supabase key that only exists server-side. It runs as a Worker, via
 * the OpenNext adapter, which is Cloudflare's supported path for a
 * full Next.js application.
 *
 * WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT.
 *
 * No R2 incremental-cache binding. The adapter's docs recommend an R2
 * cache for a site that uses `revalidate` (this one does: 60 on the
 * layout, 300 on /cases), but R2 is not enabled on the account and
 * enabling it is a billing decision that belongs to the owner. Without
 * it, revalidated pages are simply rendered per request — correct, and
 * slower than it needs to be. The site is a brochure with a calculator,
 * not a high-traffic app, so this is an optimisation to revisit rather
 * than a launch blocker. Same reasoning for the Images binding.
 *
 * `run_worker_first` on /media is NOT an optimisation, it is a
 * security fix — see the comment on it below.
 */
{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "dockentra-website",
  "main": ".open-next/worker.js",

  /**
   * 2025-05-05 or later is required by the adapter: earlier dates hit
   * a FinalizationRegistry error in the Workers runtime.
   */
  "compatibility_date": "2025-05-05",

  /**
   * nodejs_compat: the app uses node:crypto (timing-safe comparison of
   * the admin token, HMAC verification of the WhatsApp webhook) and
   * node:path. global_fetch_strictly_public is required by the adapter
   * so that fetches to our own public hostname go out through the
   * internet rather than looping back inside the Worker.
   */
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat", "global_fetch_strictly_public"],

  "assets": {
    "directory": ".open-next/assets",
    "binding": "ASSETS",

    /**
     * No `run_worker_first`. It was here to force /media through the
     * Worker so next.config.ts's cache-control rule would apply, but
     * public/_headers sets that rule directly on the asset response —
     * which is the documented mechanism, costs no Worker invocation
     * per image, and covers the security headers at the same time.
     *
     * Those security headers are the reason public/_headers exists at
     * all: Workers Assets serves a matching static file BEFORE the
     * Worker runs, so nothing under /public was receiving the headers
     * next.config.ts sets. Measured, not assumed — an HTML route came
     * back with 6 and /brand/*.png with 0.
     */
    "not_found_handling": "none"
  },

  /**
   * The Next incremental cache. NEXT_INC_CACHE_KV is the binding name
   * the adapter looks for — it is not free-form. See open-next.config.ts
   * for why this is KV rather than R2 or the read-only assets cache,
   * and for the 500 that its absence caused.
   */
  "kv_namespaces": [
    {
      "binding": "NEXT_INC_CACHE_KV",
      "id": "462a119c87504dd79773fb2fedd21edb"
    }
  ],

  /**
   * NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL IS NEEDED AT RUNTIME, NOT ONLY AT BUILD TIME,
   * AND THE FIRST PRODUCTION DEPLOY PROVED IT THE HARD WAY.
   *
   * The build was run with this value set, and the SITEMAP and
   * ROBOTS.TXT came out correct — they are generated once, at build
   * time. Every page, however, is rendered per request, and
   * resolveSiteUrl() reads process.env at that moment. With the value
   * absent from the Worker's runtime environment it fell through to
   * its localhost fallback, and the live site served:
   *
   *   <link rel="canonical" href="http://localhost:3000/pricing">
   *   <meta property="og:url" content="http://localhost:3000">
   *
   * on every single page, while the sitemap alongside them said
   * dockentra.ie. Nothing errored. Google would have been told that
   * the canonical version of every page is a machine on nobody's
   * network.
   *
   * It lives here rather than in the dashboard so it is version
   * controlled and cannot be lost: it is a public URL, not a secret.
   * Secrets stay in `wrangler secret put` and never appear in this file.
   */
  "vars": {
    "NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL": "https://dockentra.ie"
  },

  "observability": {
    "enabled": true
  }
}
